## 8. SECURITY RULES AND ACCESS CONTROL

Implement restrictive Firestore and Storage rules.

Required principles:
- Deny access by default where appropriate.
- Public visitors can read only explicitly published public catalog records.
- Authenticated users can access their own private profile, resume analysis, roadmaps, quiz attempts, saved items, and progress.
- One user must not be able to read or edit another user's private data.
- Users must not be able to change their own role to admin.
- Only authorized administrators may create, edit, publish, unpublish, feature, or delete public catalog records.
- Validate permitted fields and data types where practical.
- Restrict resume uploads to the authenticated user's authorized storage path.
- Validate file size and allowed file types.
- Never use unrestricted public read/write rules in production.
- Never deploy rules that permit every user to read or write all documents.

The Firebase Admin SDK bypasses Firestore Security Rules. Therefore, every privileged backend function must independently authenticate the caller, authorize the requested action, validate input, and verify document ownership.

Do not trust an admin role submitted by the browser.

Implement a secure administrator bootstrap process. A trusted project owner must assign the first administrator through a protected administrative process, such as a controlled script or secure deployment-time procedure. Document the exact steps.

